{"duration_api_ms":56355,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"af30181c-7458-48de-a37d-cd53fef395a4","total_cost_usd":0.41807875000000005,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3992,"cache_read_input_tokens":2075,"output_tokens":6754,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3992,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6754,"cache_read_input_tokens":2075,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3992,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3992},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-fable-5-1":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":6754,"cacheReadInputTokens":2075,"cacheCreationInputTokens":3992,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.41807875000000005,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-fable-5-1","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"claims\":[{\"text\":\"Priya Raghunathan is an author of the document \\\"Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\\\".\",\"line\":3,\"span\":\"Author: Priya Raghunathan\"},{\"text\":\"The document \\\"Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\\\" was updated on 2026-03-18.\",\"line\":4,\"span\":\"Updated: 2026-03-18\"},{\"text\":\"Devin Okonkwo is an author of the document \\\"Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\\\".\",\"line\":6,\"span\":\"Author: Devin Okonkwo\"},{\"text\":\"The document \\\"Nimbrel Ledger append rejected with HTTP 429\\\" was updated on 2026-04-11.\",\"line\":7,\"span\":\"Updated: 2026-04-11\"},{\"text\":\"Append requests to Nimbrel Ledger come back with HTTP 429 `Too Many Requests`.\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"Append requests to Nimbrel Ledger come back with HTTP 429 `Too Many Requests`.\"},{\"text\":\"When Nimbrel Ledger append requests are refused with HTTP 429, Nimbrel Relay stalls.\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"Nimbrel Relay stalls\"},{\"text\":\"When Nimbrel Ledger append requests are refused with HTTP 429, batch loaders resend the same envelope.\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"batch loaders resend the same envelope\"},{\"text\":\"When Nimbrel Ledger append requests are refused with HTTP 429, reads against the same shard begin to time out.\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"reads against the same shard begin to time out\"},{\"text\":\"The Nimbrel Ledger HTTP 429 append refusal reaches the client.\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"The refusal reaches the client and is written to the Relay and Collector logs as well.\"},{\"text\":\"The Nimbrel Ledger HTTP 429 append refusal is written to the Relay and Collector logs.\",\"line\":11,\"span\":\"The refusal reaches the client and is written to the Relay and Collector logs as well.\"},{\"text\":\"On a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment (NMD), the refusals can show up as a share of HTTP `429` replies that climbs over a fortnight, as counted at the front proxy.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"On a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment (NMD), the refusals can show up as a share of HTTP `429` replies that climbs over a fortnight, as counted at the front proxy.\"},{\"text\":\"The Nimbrel Ledger returns HTTP `429` on a growing share of requests when it refuses work rather than queueing it.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"The Ledger returns HTTP `429` on a growing share of requests when it refuses work rather than queueing it.\"},{\"text\":\"On a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment, front proxy counters agree with the client reports of HTTP 429 refusals.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"Front proxy counters agree with the client reports, so the refusals are real and not a client-side accounting error.\"},{\"text\":\"The HTTP 429 refusals on a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment are real and not a client-side accounting error.\",\"line\":13,\"span\":\"Front proxy counters agree with the client reports, so the refusals are real and not a client-side accounting error.\"},{\"text\":\"In the example refusal envelope from a Nimbrel Ledger batched append, the append batch was refused after 0 of 64 envelopes.\",\"line\":18,\"span\":\"append batch refused after 0 of 64 envelopes\"},{\"text\":\"In the example Nimbrel Ledger refusal envelope, the error type is nbl_queue_refused_exception.\",\"line\":18,\"span\":\"\\\"type\\\":\\\"nbl_queue_refused_exception\\\"\"},{\"text\":\"In the example Nimbrel Ledger refusal envelope, the reason is a refused append on ingest path.\",\"line\":18,\"span\":\"\\\"reason\\\":\\\"refused append on ingest path\"},{\"text\":\"In the example Nimbrel Ledger refusal envelope, ingest_and_leader_bytes=88121344.\",\"line\":18,\"span\":\"ingest_and_leader_bytes=88121344\"},{\"text\":\"In the example Nimbrel Ledger refusal envelope, follower_bytes=212992.\",\"line\":18,\"span\":\"follower_bytes=212992\"},{\"text\":\"In the example Nimbrel Ledger refusal envelope, all_bytes=88334336.\",\"line\":18,\"span\":\"all_bytes=88334336\"},{\"text\":\"In the example Nimbrel Ledger refusal envelope, ingest_op_bytes=155648.\",\"line\":18,\"span\":\"ingest_op_bytes=155648\"},{\"text\":\"In the example Nimbrel Ledger refusal envelope, max_ingest_bytes=88080384.\",\"line\":18,\"span\":\"max_ingest_bytes=88080384\"},{\"text\":\"In the example Nimbrel Ledger refusal envelope, the status is 429.\",\"line\":18,\"span\":\"\\\"status\\\":429\"},{\"text\":\"The Ledger operations handbook covers refused appends under Refused Appends.\",\"line\":21,\"span\":\"The Ledger operations handbook now covers the same ground under Refused Appends, with a walkthrough for each of the three refusal paths set out below.\"},{\"text\":\"The Ledger operations handbook has a walkthrough for each of the three refusal paths.\",\"line\":21,\"span\":\"The Ledger operations handbook now covers the same ground under Refused Appends, with a walkthrough for each of the three refusal paths set out below.\"},{\"text\":\"Every Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append with HTTP 429.\",\"line\":25,\"span\":\"Every Nimbrel Ledger release on every platform can refuse an append this way.\"},{\"text\":\"The rising refusal rate on a managed deployment is observed with the product Nimbrel Ledger.\",\"line\":29,\"span\":\"Product: Nimbrel Ledger\"},{\"text\":\"The rising refusal rate on a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment is observed on Version: 4.4, 5.x.\",\"line\":30,\"span\":\"Version: 4.4, 5.x\"},{\"text\":\"The rising refusal rate on a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment is observed on the platform Nimbrel Cloud.\",\"line\":31,\"span\":\"Platform: Nimbrel Cloud\"},{\"text\":\"The rising refusal rate on a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment is observed on the deployment type Managed Ledger Service (MLS).\",\"line\":32,\"span\":\"Deployment: Managed Ledger Service (MLS)\"},{\"text\":\"The rising refusal rate on a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment is observed in a production environment.\",\"line\":33,\"span\":\"Production Environment: Yes\"},{\"text\":\"A `429 - Too Many Requests` reply is what a Nimbrel Ledger node sends once it has nowhere left to put the work.\",\"line\":37,\"span\":\"A [`429 - Too Many Requests` reply](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/http-status-codes) is what a Ledger node sends once it has nowhere left to put the work, which is to say once [an append queue or a read queue is full](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/why-appends-are-refused).\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger node sends a `429 - Too Many Requests` reply once an append queue or a read queue is full.\",\"line\":37,\"span\":\"A [`429 - Too Many Requests` reply](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/http-status-codes) is what a Ledger node sends once it has nowhere left to put the work, which is to say once [an append queue or a read queue is full](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/why-appends-are-refused).\"},{\"text\":\"There are 3 ways a Nimbrel Ledger append draws a 429.\",\"line\":39,\"span\":\"There are 3 ways an append draws a 429:\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger append draws a 429 when the `append` or `system_append` worker pools hold more batches than they have slots for.\",\"line\":41,\"span\":\"The `append` or `system_append` worker pools hold more batches than they have slots for (`nbctl pool status --all`)\"},{\"text\":\"The command `nbctl pool status --all` shows the state of the Nimbrel Ledger `append` and `system_append` worker pools.\",\"line\":41,\"span\":\"The `append` or `system_append` worker pools hold more batches than they have slots for (`nbctl pool status --all`)\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger append draws a 429 when the ingest memory guard has refused the batch.\",\"line\":42,\"span\":\"The ingest memory guard has refused the batch (`nbctl guard report ingest --counters`).\"},{\"text\":\"The command `nbctl guard report ingest --counters` reports on the Nimbrel Ledger ingest memory guard.\",\"line\":42,\"span\":\"The ingest memory guard has refused the batch (`nbctl guard report ingest --counters`).\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger append draws a 429 when a circuit breaker has tripped.\",\"line\":43,\"span\":\"A circuit breaker has tripped (`nbctl breaker list --tripped`) - a breaker trips on any operation and is not particular to appends.\"},{\"text\":\"The command `nbctl breaker list --tripped` lists tripped Nimbrel Ledger circuit breakers.\",\"line\":43,\"span\":\"A circuit breaker has tripped (`nbctl breaker list --tripped`) - a breaker trips on any operation and is not particular to appends.\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger circuit breaker trips on any operation and is not particular to appends.\",\"line\":43,\"span\":\"a breaker trips on any operation and is not particular to appends\"},{\"text\":\"From release 4.6 and release 5.1 onward, appending to a `wide_text` column in Nimbrel Ledger draws a 429 on its own account whenever the batch would otherwise have run the node out of memory.\",\"line\":45,\"span\":\"**From release 4.6 and release 5.1 onward**, appending to a `wide_text` column draws a 429 on its own account whenever the batch would otherwise have run the node out of memory.\"},{\"text\":\"On a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment with a rising refusal rate, the evidence points at expensive queries and deep grouping.\",\"line\":47,\"span\":\"On a managed deployment with a rising refusal rate, the evidence points at expensive queries and deep grouping:\"},{\"text\":\"On a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment with a rising refusal rate, a handful of queries run for as long as 2 seconds each.\",\"line\":49,\"span\":\"a handful of queries run for as long as 2 seconds each\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger worker slot is unavailable for as long as an expensive query holds it.\",\"line\":49,\"span\":\"A worker slot is unavailable for as long as one of them holds it, and the queue behind it grows.\"},{\"text\":\"The queue behind a Nimbrel Ledger worker slot held by an expensive query grows.\",\"line\":49,\"span\":\"A worker slot is unavailable for as long as one of them holds it, and the queue behind it grows.\"},{\"text\":\"On a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment with a rising refusal rate, grouping on `channel.id` yields a very large number of groups.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"grouping on `channel.id` yields a very large number of groups\"},{\"text\":\"The memory cost of grouping on `channel.id` in Nimbrel Ledger is charged to the same pool the append path draws on.\",\"line\":50,\"span\":\"the memory that costs is charged to the same pool the append path draws on\"},{\"text\":\"On a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment with a rising refusal rate, processor load looks unremarkable while memory sits high.\",\"line\":51,\"span\":\"processor load looks unremarkable while memory sits high\"},{\"text\":\"On a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment with a rising refusal rate, the disagreement between processor load and memory points at query cost rather than at an undersized cluster.\",\"line\":51,\"span\":\"The two do not agree, which points at query cost rather than at an undersized cluster.\"},{\"text\":\"The workaround for Nimbrel Ledger append refusals is to take append and query load off the cluster for long enough that the queues drain and the nodes fall back under their limits.\",\"line\":55,\"span\":\"Take append and query load off the cluster for long enough that the queues drain and the nodes fall back under their limits.\"},{\"text\":\"Where batches carry `wide_text` columns and the Nimbrel Ledger cluster runs release 4.6 or release 5.1 or later, the workaround is to send fewer envelopes per batch.\",\"line\":57,\"span\":\"Where batches carry `wide_text` columns and the cluster runs release 4.6 or release 5.1 or later, send fewer envelopes per batch.\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger cluster that refuses appends has been given more work than its hardware can carry.\",\"line\":61,\"span\":\"A cluster that refuses appends has been given more work than its hardware can carry, so the answer is hardware: larger nodes (scale up), or more of them (scale out).\"},{\"text\":\"The resolution for a Nimbrel Ledger cluster that refuses appends is hardware: larger nodes (scale up), or more of them (scale out).\",\"line\":61,\"span\":\"A cluster that refuses appends has been given more work than its hardware can carry, so the answer is hardware: larger nodes (scale up), or more of them (scale out).\"},{\"text\":\"Per the Nimbrel Ledger sizing notes, scale up first in most cases.\",\"line\":61,\"span\":\"Scale up first in most cases, and scale out when what is wanted is a further copy of the data for availability, per [the sizing notes](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/sizing).\"},{\"text\":\"Per the Nimbrel Ledger sizing notes, scale out when what is wanted is a further copy of the data for availability.\",\"line\":61,\"span\":\"Scale up first in most cases, and scale out when what is wanted is a further copy of the data for availability, per [the sizing notes](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/sizing).\"},{\"text\":\"Splitting a hot stream over more leader shards in Nimbrel Ledger spreads append load across more nodes.\",\"line\":63,\"span\":\"Splitting a hot stream over more leader shards spreads append load across more nodes, which helps in some layouts.\"},{\"text\":\"Splitting a hot stream over more leader shards in Nimbrel Ledger helps in some layouts.\",\"line\":63,\"span\":\"Splitting a hot stream over more leader shards spreads append load across more nodes, which helps in some layouts.\"},{\"text\":\"For `wide_text` columns on Nimbrel Ledger release 4.6 and release 5.1 and later, the first step is cutting the number of envelopes in each append batch.\",\"line\":67,\"span\":\"Begin by cutting the number of envelopes in each append batch.\"},{\"text\":\"For `wide_text` columns on Nimbrel Ledger release 4.6 and release 5.1 and later, where smaller batches do not clear the refusals, the second step is to add processor and memory capacity to the nodes.\",\"line\":68,\"span\":\"Where smaller batches do not clear it, add processor and memory capacity to the nodes.\"},{\"text\":\"For `wide_text` columns on Nimbrel Ledger release 4.6 and release 5.1 and later, raising the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting is the last step, after cutting batch size and adding capacity.\",\"line\":69,\"span\":\"Only then raise the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting, which defaults to 10% of the heap.\"},{\"text\":\"The Nimbrel Ledger `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting defaults to 10% of the heap.\",\"line\":69,\"span\":\"Only then raise the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` cluster setting, which defaults to 10% of the heap.\"},{\"text\":\"A higher `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` lets a Nimbrel Ledger node hold more in-flight append memory before refusing.\",\"line\":69,\"span\":\"A higher ceiling lets a node hold more in-flight append memory before refusing, and a node holding too much runs out of memory instead of refusing, which is the worse outcome.\"},{\"text\":\"A Nimbrel Ledger node holding too much in-flight append memory runs out of memory instead of refusing.\",\"line\":69,\"span\":\"A higher ceiling lets a node hold more in-flight append memory before refusing, and a node holding too much runs out of memory instead of refusing, which is the worse outcome.\"},{\"text\":\"The Nimbrel Ledger `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` setting is read at startup.\",\"line\":69,\"span\":\"The setting is read at startup, so the cluster must be restarted for a change to take.\"},{\"text\":\"The Nimbrel Ledger cluster must be restarted for a change to the `ingest_guard.memory.leader.ceiling` setting to take.\",\"line\":69,\"span\":\"The setting is read at startup, so the cluster must be restarted for a change to take.\"},{\"text\":\"Where scaling and setting changes are not available for a Nimbrel Ledger cluster that refuses appends, the load itself is what changes: fewer appends, cheaper queries, or both.\",\"line\":71,\"span\":\"Where none of that is open to you, the load itself is what changes: fewer appends, cheaper queries, or both.\"},{\"text\":\"Nimbrel Ledger refusals that show up only during a spike usually clear on their own once the queues drain.\",\"line\":71,\"span\":\"Refusals that show up only during a spike usually clear on their own once the queues drain.\"},{\"text\":\"For a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment whose refusals trace to query cost, a proposed action is to lower the slow query threshold to 1 second.\",\"line\":75,\"span\":\"**Lower the slow query threshold**: set it to 1 second, so that the queries actually responsible are recorded instead of averaged away.\"},{\"text\":\"Setting the Nimbrel Ledger slow query threshold to 1 second causes the queries actually responsible to be recorded instead of averaged away.\",\"line\":75,\"span\":\"**Lower the slow query threshold**: set it to 1 second, so that the queries actually responsible are recorded instead of averaged away.\"},{\"text\":\"For a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment whose refusals trace to query cost, a proposed action is to go through the expensive queries with the team that wrote them.\",\"line\":76,\"span\":\"**Review what the queries are for**: go through the expensive ones with the team that wrote them, several of which look like they could ask for less.\"},{\"text\":\"Several of the expensive queries on the managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment look like they could ask for less.\",\"line\":76,\"span\":\"several of which look like they could ask for less\"},{\"text\":\"For a managed Nimbrel Ledger deployment whose refusals trace to query cost, a proposed action is to take a thread dump and a heap snapshot while the refusal rate is high.\",\"line\":77,\"span\":\"**Capture evidence during a refusal**: while the refusal rate is high, take a thread dump and a heap snapshot rather than reasoning from counters alone.\"},{\"text\":\"Internal ticket NB-40917 is located at https://tickets.nimbrel.example/issues/40917.\",\"line\":83,\"span\":\"[Internal ticket NB-40917](https://tickets.nimbrel.example/issues/40917)\"},{\"text\":\"The Ledger slow query log documentation is located at https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/slow-query-log.\",\"line\":87,\"span\":\"[Ledger slow query log](https://docs.nimbrel.example/ledger/slow-query-log)\"},{\"text\":\"The HTTP 429 replies knowledge article is located at https://support.nimbrel.example/knowledge/318kqp2.\",\"line\":88,\"span\":\"[HTTP 429 replies](https://support.nimbrel.example/knowledge/318kqp2)\"}]}","ttft_ms":58203,"type":"result","duration_ms":58242,"uuid":"ec212a96-30a5-4abd-9d2d-e8d95250aabb","ttft_stream_ms":4098,"time_to_request_ms":1885,"first_content_frame_ms":4098,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
